From axioms (1-5) one can check for example that $0 < 1$: otherwise $1 < 0$, so $0 = 1 - 1 < 0 - 1 = -1$, hence $0 = 0 dot (-1) < (-1) dot (-1) = 1$, a contradiction. The rationals sit inside $RR$ by identifying $(a)/(b) in QQ$ with $a times b^(-1)$, but $QQ$ itself does not satisfy axiom (6): the set ${p in QQ : p > 0, p^2 < 2}$ has no least upper bound in $QQ$. In (6) it is crucial that $S$ is non-empty and bounded above; dropping either assumption destroys the existence of a least upper bound.
